{"hash_algorithm":"sha256","ts":"2026-11-16T23:29:01.541Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"9a30eec154ff7d7937b394af","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":{"value":"implementer","source":"inferred","confidence":"medium","rule_version":"v2","evidence":["claim:pm-3627y1","claim:pm-3fbj1f","claim:pm-ycgb5g","lineage:pm-3fbj1f","lineage:pm-qwoy","lineage:pm-doxj"]},"topic":{"value":"workset:pm-3627y1+pm-3fbj1f+pm-ycgb5g","source":"inferred","confidence":"medium","rule_version":"v2","evidence":["claim:pm-3627y1","claim:pm-3fbj1f","claim:pm-ycgb5g","lineage:pm-3fbj1f","lineage:pm-qwoy","lineage:pm-doxj"]},"version":{"value":"2.1.326","source":"probe"}},"op":"comment_add","patch":[{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-11-16T23:29:01.541Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/comments","value":[{"created_at":"2026-11-16T23:29:01.541Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"Implementation (branch fix/secret-guard-grammar-and-payload-keys): scanMutationSecrets now runs two passes, scanStringLeaves (unchanged) and scanPayloadMembers. The second pass visits every object member whose value is a string or a container. It splits camelCase and PascalCase words with _ (accessToken reads as access_Token, HTTPToken as HTTP_Token), keeps only the key-named rules whose declared structured key vocabulary ends the name (password_assignment, high_entropy_assignment, credential_key_assignment), and judges the member exactly as its JSON spelling {\"<name>\": <value>}. Only a finding at the synthesized key counts, so a credential inside the value stays the leaf scan's own finding; the finding reports the member path, never the value. Numbers, booleans and null under a credential key are not judged, so token_budget: 800 and other pm numeric fields stay clean, and token_budget, max_tokens, tokenBudget, secretGuard, upsertKey and idempotency_key never match a key vocabulary. Repeated objects serialize as null and BigInts as decimal text, so no value type makes the scan fail open. The pm-gh1704 must-allow corpus holds for object payloads for every value that is data (35 entries in the spec). Three corpus entries (getToken(options), config.auth?.token, DEFAULT_TOKEN) are clean only as unquoted code; as string values they are literals, and their JSON spelling {\"token\":\"...\"} is already flagged on main (verified before and after the change). The spec pins that a member gets the verdict of its JSON spelling. Behavior change to note: a token-named member holding a GitHub token now reports github_token plus credential_key_assignment and high_entropy_assignment, as its JSON spelling does; the contract test in tests/unit/sdk/mutation-guard.spec.ts was updated accordingly.\n"}]}],"before_hash":"8b7a7ae44d0e4305ddab7d14301ec3ea62d15d708d99d3c44def3c2524c40343","after_hash":"d5b450c5d864b06acaed40932b234644461ebec67d92f0279569ea220127d9b1","item_hash_version":3,"context":{"agent_provenance_outcomes":{"model":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"},"version":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"}}},"event_class":"substantive","record_hash_version":1,"record_hash":"0ecdefbb2fda911192aa90e5052b817df2cfcbbfbd37208c03d24d15f4e9f480"}
{"hash_algorithm":"sha256","ts":"2026-11-17T00:12:21.969Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"9a30eec154ff7d7937b394af","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":{"value":"implementer","source":"inferred","confidence":"medium","rule_version":"v2","evidence":["claim:pm-3627y1","claim:pm-3fbj1f","claim:pm-ycgb5g","lineage:pm-3fbj1f","lineage:pm-qwoy","lineage:pm-doxj"]},"topic":{"value":"workset:pm-3627y1+pm-3fbj1f+pm-ycgb5g","source":"inferred","confidence":"medium","rule_version":"v2","evidence":["claim:pm-3627y1","claim:pm-3fbj1f","claim:pm-ycgb5g","lineage:pm-3fbj1f","lineage:pm-qwoy","lineage:pm-doxj"]},"version":{"value":"2.1.326","source":"probe"}},"op":"comment_add","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/comments/1","value":{"created_at":"2026-11-17T00:12:21.969Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"Codex review round 1 (gpt-6.1-sol, xhigh) found four problems in the first member pass, all fixed by a redesign: (1) high: the pass serialized container values with JSON.stringify, so a boxed BigInt or a throwing toJSON on any neighbor threw inside the scan and the guard failed open, dropping a real finding even in block mode; (2) a chain of credential-named members ({token:{token:...}}) re-serialized and re-scanned every subtree, so the work was quadratic in depth (about 290 ms for 800 levels); (3) camelCase splitting broke names the rules already read (passPhrase became pass_Phrase, APIkey became AP_Ikey); (4) a shared object first seen under a plain key was never judged again under a credential key. Redesign: scanPayloadMembers walks the payload once and judges each string leaf with the rule's own value judge. The new acceptsStructuredValue in secret-rules.ts runs createValueJudge on the JSON spelling of that one string. Key shapes come from the new structuredKeyShape, which reuses the rule's compiled key pattern and keyShape. A leaf is judged leniently when its own exact key names it or when it is an array element under some exact key, and compactly otherwise when any credential key names it or an ancestor, which mirrors how findStructuredSpellingMatches and walkContainer judge the payload's JSON spelling. Names are read both as written and camelCase-split, and a shared object is revisited only under a context signature it has not been judged in. Findings now report the leaf path ($.password[0], $.password.value) instead of the container path, matching the acceptance criterion. Proof: an exhaustive grid of 10,432 one-leaf payloads (9 container shapes up to three levels x 8 keys per level x 8 values) gives the same rule set as scanning the payload's JSON text; five mutants of the judgment rules (exact judged compact, array elements under exact judged compact, object members under a named ancestor unjudged, exact and named context not inherited) each fail that test. A first random-sample version compared whole-payload rule sets and let the first mutant survive, because another leaf in the same payload often produced the same rule; the grid uses exactly one string leaf per payload. A 1,000-level credential-named chain now scans in a few milliseconds. Follow-up filed: pm-wq9njf (the pre-existing recursive leaf walk overflows the stack near 2,000 levels and the guard then fails open).\n"}},{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-11-17T00:12:21.969Z"}],"before_hash":"d5b450c5d864b06acaed40932b234644461ebec67d92f0279569ea220127d9b1","after_hash":"287dc074723806bb828cb2fda5c92ccb1afcd1828eeba14225b777cfadc0de36","item_hash_version":3,"context":{"agent_provenance_outcomes":{"model":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"},"version":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"}}},"event_class":"substantive","record_hash_version":1,"record_hash":"418808555f3a33747189db1e36b47d86d032e3755472f152c6d5c790bd4e9351"}
{"hash_algorithm":"sha256","ts":"2026-11-17T00:41:10.701Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"9a30eec154ff7d7937b394af","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":{"value":"implementer","source":"inferred","confidence":"medium","rule_version":"v2","evidence":["claim:pm-3627y1","claim:pm-3fbj1f","claim:pm-wq9njf","claim:pm-ycgb5g","lineage:pm-wq9njf","lineage:pm-qwoy","lineage:pm-doxj"]},"topic":{"value":"workset:pm-3627y1+pm-3fbj1f+pm-wq9njf+pm-ycgb5g","source":"inferred","confidence":"medium","rule_version":"v2","evidence":["claim:pm-3627y1","claim:pm-3fbj1f","claim:pm-wq9njf","claim:pm-ycgb5g","lineage:pm-wq9njf","lineage:pm-qwoy","lineage:pm-doxj"]},"version":{"value":"2.1.326","source":"probe"}},"op":"comment_add","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/comments/2","value":{"created_at":"2026-11-17T00:41:10.701Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"Codex review round 2 (gpt-6.1-sol, xhigh) on the redesigned pass reported three issues. Two are fixed. (1) camelCase compound keys: dbPassPhrase and clientAPIkey escaped both the written and the fully split reading. nameSpellings now adds readings that split only the prefix before each of the last three word boundaries (dbPassPhrase reads as db_PassPhrase, clientAPIkey as client_APIkey). The number of readings stays bounded, so reading a name stays linear; tokenBudgetMaxTokens and secretGuardMode stay clean. (2) A quoted delimiter inside a command substitution, belonging to pm-ycgb5g's reference grammar, is fixed there. The third issue (references nested three or more levels in an unquoted value) is a documented limit of the declared regex grammar, which follows one nesting level; deeper nesting is followed inside quoted values and elements. It is recorded as a residual on pm-jcz7qz's adversarial corpus.\n"}},{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-11-17T00:41:10.701Z"}],"before_hash":"287dc074723806bb828cb2fda5c92ccb1afcd1828eeba14225b777cfadc0de36","after_hash":"fbeeef1c124f3a4fd90143fff7a776aea6886dbbf3f85187d526bbac32565774","item_hash_version":3,"context":{"agent_provenance_outcomes":{"model":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"},"version":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"}}},"event_class":"substantive","record_hash_version":1,"record_hash":"65b4adf6a755fda7a80afbc966b403b6f36903967830c1c2cb698c63df232f36"}
